\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces DFS encoded subspace projection. Each encoded qubit is defined by three physical electron spins. The projection $\Pi $ maps the physical Hilbert space ($2^{3n}$ dimensions) to the encoded subspace ($2^n$ dimensions), reducing the memory requirement for 25 encoded qubits from an intractable $2^{75}$ to a manageable $2^{25}$ (512~MB).}}{6}{figure.2}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:dfs}{{2}{6}{DFS encoded subspace projection. Each encoded qubit is defined by three physical electron spins. The projection $\Pi $ maps the physical Hilbert space ($2^{3n}$ dimensions) to the encoded subspace ($2^n$ dimensions), reducing the memory requirement for 25 encoded qubits from an intractable $2^{75}$ to a manageable $2^{25}$ (512~MB)}{figure.2}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3}{\ignorespaces Multi-scale SLEDGE grid architectures supported by SiliQun. Each node represents a DFS-encoded qubit defined by three physical electron spins (shown as three dots within each circle); lines indicate inter-qubit exchange couplings. Panels (a)--(c) show configurations benchmarked on a single A100 GPU: the 3$\times $3 grid (9 encoded qubits, 27 spins), the 4$\times $4 grid (16 encoded qubits, 48 spins), and the 5$\times $5 grid (25 encoded qubits, 75 spins; 430~ms per 100-gate episode). Panel (d) shows the 6$\times $6 grid (36 encoded qubits, 108 spins), which exceeds A100 memory but is projected to be feasible on an H200 GPU ($\sim $110~s per episode). The scaling analysis in Section~\ref {sec:limitations} provides further detail on memory and timing constraints.}}{7}{figure.3}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:sledge}{{3}{7}{Multi-scale SLEDGE grid architectures supported by SiliQun. Each node represents a DFS-encoded qubit defined by three physical electron spins (shown as three dots within each circle); lines indicate inter-qubit exchange couplings. Panels (a)--(c) show configurations benchmarked on a single A100 GPU: the 3$\times $3 grid (9 encoded qubits, 27 spins), the 4$\times $4 grid (16 encoded qubits, 48 spins), and the 5$\times $5 grid (25 encoded qubits, 75 spins; 430~ms per 100-gate episode). Panel (d) shows the 6$\times $6 grid (36 encoded qubits, 108 spins), which exceeds A100 memory but is projected to be feasible on an H200 GPU ($\sim $110~s per episode). The scaling analysis in Section~\ref {sec:limitations} provides further detail on memory and timing constraints}{figure.3}{}}
