\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {7}{\ignorespaces SiliQun scaling analysis. (a) Memory requirements for the DFS-projected state vector (blue) versus the full physical Hilbert space (red) as a function of encoded qubit count. Horizontal lines indicate GPU memory capacities; markers show the maximum encoded qubit count for each configuration. The red star marks the Osaka/Fixstars result (41 qubits, full space, 1,024 GPUs). (b) Estimated wall time for $10^6$ DRL training episodes on a single A100 GPU, with feasibility zones indicated. The practical DRL training limit on a single GPU is approximately 27--28 encoded qubits.}}{15}{figure.7}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:scaling}{{7}{15}{SiliQun scaling analysis. (a) Memory requirements for the DFS-projected state vector (blue) versus the full physical Hilbert space (red) as a function of encoded qubit count. Horizontal lines indicate GPU memory capacities; markers show the maximum encoded qubit count for each configuration. The red star marks the Osaka/Fixstars result (41 qubits, full space, 1,024 GPUs). (b) Estimated wall time for $10^6$ DRL training episodes on a single A100 GPU, with feasibility zones indicated. The practical DRL training limit on a single GPU is approximately 27--28 encoded qubits}{figure.7}{}}
